= Metaphysics =

'''Metaphysics''' is the branch of philosophy concerned with the most general structure of reality: what exists, what kinds of things exist, how entities persist and depend on one another, what makes possibilities possible, what causation is, and what it means for some facts or entities to be more fundamental than others.

Metaphysics is not synonymous with mysticism, spirituality, or supernatural claims. Contemporary analytic metaphysics often uses logic, conceptual analysis, ordinary-language evidence, and results from physics, biology, mathematics, and other sciences to evaluate competing accounts of reality.<ref name="sep">[https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaphysics/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Metaphysics.”]</ref>

== Historical background ==

The name derives from the traditional placement of Aristotle's writings now called the ''Metaphysics'' after his works on physics.

Aristotle studied being, substance, form, matter, cause, potentiality, actuality, and first principles.

Later metaphysics developed through:

* medieval scholastic philosophy;
* early modern debates about substance and mind;
* Kant's critique of speculative metaphysics;
* nineteenth- and twentieth-century idealism, pragmatism, phenomenology, and analytic philosophy.

Contemporary metaphysics is highly pluralistic.

There is no single agreed metaphysical system.

== Ontology ==

'''Ontology''' studies what exists and what categories of entities reality contains.

Questions include:

* Are there numbers?
* Do properties exist?
* Are possible worlds real?
* Are events distinct from objects?
* Are social institutions real entities?
* Do composite objects exist beyond their parts?

An ontological theory commits to some inventory of entities or categories.

Ontology is a major part of metaphysics but does not exhaust it.

== Existence ==

Philosophers distinguish questions such as:

* What does it mean to exist?
* Is existence a property?
* Are there different modes of being?
* What are the ontological commitments of a theory?

In formal logic, existential quantification is commonly represented by:

<math>\exists x\,P(x).</math>

But the philosophical interpretation of quantification and ontological commitment remains debated.

== Categories ==

Metaphysical categories can include:

* objects;
* properties;
* relations;
* events;
* processes;
* facts;
* states of affairs.

Different theories divide reality differently.

A process ontology may treat processes as fundamental where a substance ontology treats enduring objects as primary.

The choice should be argued for rather than inferred from grammar alone.

== Universals and particulars ==

A '''particular''' is an individual entity such as a specific apple.

A '''universal''' is a repeatable property or relation such as redness, if such entities exist.

Major positions include:

'''Realism''' — universals exist in some sense.

'''Nominalism''' — only particulars exist; generality is explained without universals.

'''Trope theory''' — properties are particularized instances, such as this apple's particular redness.

The debate concerns what best explains similarity, predication, and laws.

== Identity ==

Identity is commonly governed by Leibniz's law:

<math>x=y\Rightarrow\forall P(P(x)\leftrightarrow P(y)).</math>

If two entities are identical, they cannot differ in genuine properties.

Metaphysical problems arise when objects change through time.

A ship can replace all its planks.

A person can undergo physical change.

What makes something the same entity across change is a problem of persistence.

== Persistence ==

Two broad families are:

'''Endurantism''' — objects are wholly present at each time at which they exist.

'''Perdurantism''' — objects persist by having different temporal parts.

Other theories include stage theory and process-based accounts.

These frameworks respond differently to puzzles about change, coincidence, and temporal identity.

== Mereology ==

'''Mereology''' is the theory of parts and wholes.

Questions include:

* When do several objects compose a larger object?
* Can two objects occupy the same region by sharing parts?
* Is composition unrestricted?

Mereology can be studied formally.

Its philosophical interpretation remains contested.

== Modality ==

'''Modality''' concerns possibility and necessity.

A proposition is necessary if it could not have been otherwise.

It is possible if it could have been true.

Modal logic uses:

<math>\Box p</math>

for “necessarily <math>p</math>” and

<math>\Diamond p</math>

for “possibly <math>p</math>.”

The semantics and metaphysics of modality are separate questions.

== Possible worlds ==

Possible-world semantics evaluates modal propositions relative to alternative worlds.

Different metaphysical interpretations include:

* worlds as concrete realities;
* worlds as abstract representations;
* worlds as maximal consistent states.

David Lewis defended modal realism, under which possible worlds are concrete.

Other philosophers use possible worlds without accepting Lewis's ontology.

== Essence ==

An '''essential''' property is one an entity could not lose while remaining that entity.

For example, if an object is essentially material, it could not exist as wholly immaterial.

Essence is not always reducible to necessity.

Some contemporary theories treat essence as explanatory of necessity rather than defined by it.

== Causation ==

Metaphysics asks what causal relations are.

Major approaches include:

* regularity theories;
* counterfactual theories;
* interventionist approaches;
* causal powers;
* process theories.

Science identifies many causal relationships.

Metaphysics asks what kind of relation those findings instantiate.

No one theory of causation is universally accepted.

== Counterfactuals ==

A counterfactual has the form:

<pre>
If A had occurred, B would have occurred.
</pre>

Counterfactual reasoning is central to many causal theories.

Evaluating counterfactuals requires specifying which differences from the actual world are relevant.

Possible-world semantics provides one framework.

== Laws of nature ==

Metaphysics asks what makes a statement a law rather than an accidental regularity.

Major positions include:

* Humean best-system accounts;
* governing-law theories;
* dispositional or powers-based accounts.

Physics supplies candidate laws.

Metaphysics analyzes their status.

== Dispositions ==

A disposition is a tendency such as fragility or solubility.

A fragile glass can be fragile even if it is never struck.

Dispositional metaphysics asks whether such powers are fundamental or reducible to categorical structures and laws.

== Time ==

Metaphysical questions about time include:

* Is only the present real?
* Do past and future entities exist?
* Does time objectively pass?
* What makes temporal order?

Major views include:

'''Presentism''' — only present entities exist.

'''Eternalism''' — past, present, and future entities are equally real.

'''Growing block''' — past and present exist, but future does not yet.

Physics constrains but does not automatically settle every metaphysical interpretation.

== Space and spacetime ==

Metaphysics of space asks whether spatial relations exist independently of objects.

'''Substantivalism''' treats space or spacetime as an entity in its own right.

'''Relationism''' treats spatial facts as relations among objects or events.

Modern spacetime physics changes the terms of this debate but does not make all philosophical questions disappear.

== Grounding ==

'''Grounding''' is a proposed relation of metaphysical dependence or explanation.

A fact about a set may be grounded in facts about its members.

A social fact may be grounded partly in facts about people and institutions.

Grounding is often represented as an explanatory relation distinct from causation.<ref name="grounding">[https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/grounding/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Metaphysical Grounding.”]</ref>

There is no universal agreement that grounding is primitive or even needed.

== Fundamentality ==

A metaphysical theory can distinguish fundamental from derivative reality.

If <math>A</math> is grounded in <math>B</math>, then <math>B</math> can be treated as more fundamental in that respect.

Physicalism, for example, is often interpreted as claiming that higher-level facts depend on physical facts.

The precise dependence relation matters.

== Emergence ==

[[Emergence]] concerns higher-level properties not straightforwardly reducible to descriptions of parts.

Weak emergence can be compatible with physical dependence.

Strong emergence proposes more radical irreducibility or novel causal powers.

The metaphysical strength of emergence claims should be stated explicitly.

== Natural kinds ==

A natural kind is a category thought to reflect objective structure in nature rather than arbitrary classification.

Examples often discussed include chemical elements and biological species.

Biology complicates simple essentialist accounts because species and populations evolve.

Natural-kind theories should be fitted to actual scientific practice.

== Abstract objects ==

Numbers, sets, propositions, and mathematical structures raise questions about abstract existence.

'''Platonism''' treats some mathematical entities as mind-independent abstract objects.

'''Nominalist''' or fictionalist approaches seek alternatives.

Mathematics can be successful regardless of which metaphysical interpretation is correct.

== Social ontology ==

Social ontology studies entities such as:

* money;
* institutions;
* corporations;
* laws;
* roles.

These entities can depend on human practices while still having objective social effects.

“Socially constructed” does not mean unreal.

The dependence relation must be specified.

== Mind and body ==

The mind–body problem is partly metaphysical.

Positions include:

* physicalism;
* dualism;
* idealism;
* neutral monism.

Neuroscience constrains theories of mind.

It does not by itself define every metaphysical relation between consciousness and physical processes.

== Methodology ==

Metaphysical methods can include:

* logical analysis;
* conceptual analysis;
* thought experiments;
* inference to best explanation;
* consistency with science;
* theoretical virtues such as simplicity and unification.

Thought experiments test intuitions under described conditions.

They are not substitutes for empirical data when the question is empirical.

== Metametaphysics ==

'''Metametaphysics''' asks what metaphysical questions themselves mean and how they can be answered.

Questions include:

* Are ontological disputes substantive or verbal?
* What determines the correct quantifier domain?
* How should theoretical virtues be weighted?
* Can metaphysical claims be empirically constrained?

This field reflects disagreement not only about answers but about the method of metaphysics itself.

== Metaphysics and science ==

Metaphysics and science interact.

Scientific theories can eliminate or constrain metaphysical possibilities.

For example:

* relativity constrains theories of time;
* quantum theory affects accounts of identity and locality;
* evolutionary biology constrains essentialist species theories.

Metaphysics can analyze the ontological commitments and explanatory structure of scientific theories.

It should not override well-established empirical findings through intuition alone.

== Metaphysical versus scientific hypotheses ==

A scientific hypothesis makes empirically testable predictions.

A metaphysical thesis can concern the interpretation or structure underlying empirically equivalent theories.

Some metaphysical views can have empirical consequences.

The distinction is therefore not absolute, but evidentiary standards should match the claim.

== Epistemic boundary ==

Metaphysics asks broad structural questions that often exceed what direct measurement alone can decide.

This does not make metaphysical claims immune to evidence.

A responsible metaphysical theory should be:

* logically coherent;
* clear about commitments;
* compatible with relevant science;
* explicit about what evidence could count for or against it.

